WEBVTT

00:00:00.159 --> 00:00:00.968
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 3

00:00:01.483 --> 00:00:03.032
Life in prison

00:00:03.873 --> 00:00:06.876
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Modern prisons often hold thousands of inmates,

00:00:07.074 --> 00:00:12.457
and prison design now aims to limit the movement of prisoners around the facility.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.859
Instead of large blocks of cells,

00:00:15.922 --> 00:00:19.743
smaller modules are arranged around an outdoor exercise yard

00:00:20.131 --> 00:00:23.923
and each module may be supervised by only one prison officer.

00:00:24.764 --> 00:00:27.942
Some prisoners may be placed in a cell on their own

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:32.195
in solitary confinement for their own safety, for example, celebrities,

00:00:32.236 --> 00:00:38.389
former police officers, and those convicted of certain types of crimes, e.g. against children.

00:00:39.087 --> 00:00:43.007
Maximum security prisons are for inmates considered dangerous,

00:00:43.032 --> 00:00:46.238
disruptive, or likely to try to escape.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:52.530
They can be locked up often for up to 23 hours a day and are under constant surveillance.

00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:56.188
At the other end of the spectrum are open prisons,

00:00:56.474 --> 00:00:59.792
which allow free movement to work or do activities;

00:01:00.284 --> 00:01:05.315
some prisoners may be allowed home leave or part-time employment outside the prison.

00:01:06.196 --> 00:01:10.607
The modern prison system intends a prison sentence to be less of a punishment

00:01:10.813 --> 00:01:12.687
and more about rehabilitation,

00:01:13.061 --> 00:01:16.842
so that the prisoner can return to normal life and not reoffend.

00:01:17.747 --> 00:01:20.347
Prisons provide healthcare and education,

00:01:20.681 --> 00:01:24.575
but also things like gyms and weightlifting rooms, sports teams,

00:01:24.638 --> 00:01:27.108
arts and crafts, and television sets.

00:01:27.997 --> 00:01:33.876
<v><b>Such services are controversial:</b></v> some people claim that prisons are ‘soft’ on inmates,

00:01:34.098 --> 00:01:38.040
while others argue that it is dehumanizing not to provide recreation.

00:01:38.961 --> 00:01:43.721
Prison administrators say that keeping prisoners occupied helps to maintain order

00:01:44.086 --> 00:01:47.539
and that prisoners can be deprived of recreation as punishment.